  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-EGY101</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacity of constituents to develop labour market services and  support transitions to decent work, particularly for youth and women</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Egypt is facing substantial demographic and structural constraints on the labour market to provide decent work to its growing working age population. 1.4 million jobs would need to be created every year until 2030 to absorb all new markets entrants. Yet challenges remain and still limit decent and productive job creation for all: the very low labour force participation of women, a constant high level of unemployment amongst the youth and a persistent high level of informal employment at around 68% (ILOSTAT) which limits enterprises to grow and create jobs as well as workers to access productive jobs and social protection. In this context, the Ministry of Labour has called the ILO in 2023 to support the formulation and implementation of the first National Employment Strategy for Egypt. Based on a thorough employment diagnosis, an advanced draft of the Strategy was elaborated with the technical support of the ILO in collaboration with over 20 Ministries, agencies and workers and employers representatives. The strategy is expected to be formally adopted during the biennium and complemented by an implementation plan that will detail actions, timeline and responsibilities. In parallel, the ILO will also continue providing technical assistance to MSMEDA to finalize the Informality strategy and its operational plan, both to be formally adopted during the biennium. ILO will also capitalize on the outputs of the recent project funded by WFP to enhance the capacities of Public Employment Services under the Ministry of Labour, which have a key role to play to orient, accompany and place women, young people and vulnerable groups into decent and productive work.</narrative>
  </description>
